      goal: { text: 'every seed scores −250 or better on the test. A mean can hide one seed that hangs.', test: (a) => (isFinite(a.summary.worstSeed) ? a.summary.worstSeed : a.summary.pendulumScore) >= -250 },
      explain: { id: 'explore-x', prompt: 'With "a bonus tuned to a target randomness", all seeds swing up. Why tune α instead of fixing it?', options: [
        o(['The right bonus depends on the size of the rewards.', 'Too large an α keeps the policy too random; too small and it collapses.', 'Tuning α to hold the entropy near a target removes that choice.'], 'The good α depends on the task; a target randomness does not.', 1,
          `Haarnoja et al. 2018b: “a sub-optimal temperature can drastically degrade performance”. In this toy a fixed α = 0.2 also worked (${i0(M('pd-sac-fixed').test)}): the pendulum is not sensitive to it.`),
        o(['A tuned α makes the policy more random.', 'More randomness finds more.', 'So it is better.'], 'More randomness.', 0, `<b>Wrong at step 1.</b> Both kept a similar spread here (${d2(M('pd-sac-auto').std)} tuned, ${d2(M('pd-sac-fixed').std)} fixed). α moves both ways, toward the target.`),
        o(['α grows during training.', 'A growing bonus forces exploration later.', 'So stuck seeds escape.'], 'A growing bonus.', 0, '<b>Wrong at step 1.</b> α moves up or down, whichever brings the entropy to its target.'),
      ] },
      soWhat: 'On a real arm, a policy that stops exploring stops improving. Watch its spread. SAC\'s tuned bonus keeps the spread from collapsing, but every random try is a real move: limit the spread near joint limits and the table.',
      cannotTeach: 'Exploration costs nothing here. On the arm, random torques near the table or a joint limit are dangerous.',
